WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.360
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:25.140
Hello.

9
00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:26.420
Hey, good morning.

10
00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:29.600
Did this episode make you want to buy a new suit?

11
00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:30.880
No, it did not.

12
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.620
Did it make you want to throw a basketball into a fish tank and electrocute a bunch of fish?

13
00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:36.280
No.

14
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:37.520
Of course not.

15
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:40.360
Did it make you want a lot of coffee?

16
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:41.820
It did not.

17
00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:45.890
I did laugh at this episode.

18
00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:47.410
I laughed a whole bunch.

19
00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:47.770
Yeah.

20
00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:54.650
It is Suit Warehouse, Season 9, Episode 11, written by Dan Graney and directed by Matt Sohn.

21
00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:55.930
Here's your summary.

22
00:00:56.150 --> 00:01:04.090
With Jim working out of town, Dwight enlists Clark's help to help play a father and son in order to land a new client.

23
00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:09.630
Daryl heads to Philly to interview with Jim's new company, and Pam tags along.

24
00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:18.570
And finally, the arrival of a new Nespresso machine throws the entire office into a caffeinated state of chaos.

25
00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:21.330
I mean, it was mayhem.

26
00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:23.470
They started taking the place apart.

27
00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:24.110
Literally.

28
00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:25.930
All right.

29
00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:27.390
Fast fact number one.

30
00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:32.050
Like I said, this episode was directed by our very own Matt Sohn.

31
00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:35.830
Yes, you guys have heard us talk about Matt Sohn for years on the show.

32
00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:36.670
Oh, yeah.

33
00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:38.230
He's been with us since the beginning.

34
00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:42.890
Matt was also the one that knew you were pregnant when you thought you were fooling everyone.

35
00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:43.590
Yeah.

36
00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:46.950
He said, Jenna, I look at you through a camera all day long.

37
00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:48.810
I could see all of it happening.

38
00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:49.470
Yes.

39
00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:51.390
And he's also a father of two.

40
00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:51.830
Yes.

41
00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:52.650
So he knew.

42
00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:53.770
He knew the signs.

43
00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:56.990
So I actually reconnected with Matt recently.

44
00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:59.670
And he, you know, Jenna, he hikes.

45
00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:01.030
He goes on all these hikes.

46
00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:07.590
I know he recently went on a hike with Chris Haston, who is Kate Flannery's longtime boyfriend

47
00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:10.850
and our NBC photographer, and David Denman.

48
00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:13.050
The three of them went on a big old hike recently.

49
00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:13.610
Oh, yeah.

50
00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.290
So he said to me, Ann, I should want to go for a hike.

51
00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:17.570
And I was like, yes.

52
00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:18.370
No.

53
00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:19.290
But.

54
00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:20.230
You don't want that.

55
00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:22.270
He goes so faSt.

56
00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:25.050
He's like, he's like sporty.

57
00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:30.250
And as it came time for the hike, I really lucked out because we got all that rain.

58
00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:31.530
Remember, L.A.

59
00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:33.650
just got like a downpour of rain.

60
00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:39.110
And so I was like, how are those hiking trails looking with my fingers crossed?

61
00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:40.390
He goes, pretty muddy.

62
00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:42.650
And he said, want to do a neighborhood walk?

63
00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:43.790
I said, yes.

64
00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:44.670
Yes.

65
00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:45.750
Oh, you lucked out.

66
00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:46.390
I lucked out.

67
00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:47.610
And, you know, we're neighbors.

68
00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:49.530
So we live in the same neighborhood.

69
00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:49.870
Yeah.

70
00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:53.010
So we just went on this wonderful walk.

71
00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:58.890
And he let me point out flowers and different like awnings I liked.

72
00:02:58.930 --> 00:02:59.870
He didn't go too fast?

73
00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:00.910
He didn't go too faSt.

74
00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:02.030
It was a stroll.

75
00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:02.710
Nice.

76
00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:04.730
Anyway, Matt is wonderful.

77
00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:09.090
And on this walk, we were talking about the podcast and he was saying how much he's enjoying

78
00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:09.390
it.

79
00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:13.190
He said, you know, when you get to Suit Warehouse, I would love to share with you some behind

80
00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:14.030
the scenes details.

81
00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:17.690
And I said, Matt, we will 100% hit you up for that.

82
00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:18.750
And he was so gracious.

83
00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:21.610
He said to send a ton of audio clips for this episode.

84
00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:22.170
He did.

85
00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:23.030
And they're great.

86
00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:24.210
He has some good details.

87
00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.990
So buckle in for that.

88
00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:26.830
All right.

89
00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:30.210
Fast fact number two, building athlete.

90
00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:31.210
Yes.

91
00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:34.130
This was a big episode in so many ways.

92
00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:38.350
But one of the biggest things in this episode is that we revealed Jim's new business for the

93
00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:39.170
first time.

94
00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:41.310
His swanky new office.

95
00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:42.170
I know.

96
00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:43.830
And it's really impressive.

97
00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:49.070
Steve Burgess said, we spent a very long time designing it and it was expensive.

98
00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:53.690
He said, but we were able to spread the cost over a bunch of different episodes.

99
00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:55.050
So.

100
00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:56.270
Some sneaky budgeting.

101
00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:57.010
I guess.

102
00:03:57.490 --> 00:04:04.210
We also asked Matt Sohn about being an athlete for the first time and having to direct this

103
00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:04.950
new space.

104
00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:07.050
And here's what he had to say.

105
00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:11.490
This episode established the athlete offices.

106
00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:16.410
They were built on our lot in our warehouse set.

107
00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:20.530
I believe Matt Flynn was the production designer at this time.

108
00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:23.290
He had taken over for Michael Gallenberg.

109
00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:28.990
Athlete was built to look just the opposite of the Dunder Mifflin set.

110
00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:33.910
It had high ceilings, lots of windows and nice views.

111
00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:39.430
It had exposed brick and the set design was also much hipper.

112
00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:42.790
There were cool desks, chairs and artwork.

113
00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:46.870
It was an alluring place for both Jim and Daryl.

114
00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:51.070
I feel like the crew only had a few weeks to build this set.

115
00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:57.550
And I think they were a little bit behind because we ended up having to shoot the scenes

116
00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:03.590
there at the end of our episode, you know, to make it fit within our schedule.

117
00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:11.930
You know, it didn't really occur to me how kind of not great the Dunder Mifflin office

118
00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:18.190
space is until Matt started describing the athlete of high ceilings, lots of windows.

119
00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:20.110
So I was like, oh, yeah.

120
00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:23.730
Well, Jenna, Matt Sohn mentioned Matt Flynn.

121
00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:27.750
And, you know, I had reached out to Matt Flynn when we broke down Pool Party to get all the

122
00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:30.810
details about Robert California's swanky pool house.

123
00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:31.210
Yes.

124
00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:34.090
And that's two swankies, Dr. Thibodeau.

125
00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:35.830
I've already said swanky twice.

126
00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:43.050
But anyway, so Matt Flynn told me, when you get to the fancy athlete offices, I want to

127
00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:43.710
share about it.

128
00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:46.950
And so here's what he had to say about building that set.

129
00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:48.230
Oh, I'm very excited.

130
00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:49.090
I'm leaning in.

131
00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:49.710
All right.

132
00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:55.510
I quote, this story arc gave the art department a great opportunity to show how many changes

133
00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:58.890
had occurred in the workplace since the office first aired.

134
00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:05.030
Slick and with much less clutter, textured with a completely different color palette, no

135
00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:10.230
stacks of file boxes in the corners, a reliance of laptops rather than clunky old equipment

136
00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:11.910
like fax machines, for example.

137
00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:16.570
And we were able to show the athlete office space in a couple of episodes where it was

138
00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:21.890
being converted from a raw, unfinished, and maybe even a little spooky environment, allowing

139
00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:26.730
us to get a grip on Jim's anxiety about making such a big career change.

140
00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:28.030
Wow.

141
00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:28.890
I know.

142
00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:30.530
I love all this detail.

143
00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:31.690
Oh, there's more.

144
00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:35.930
Matt Flynn goes on to say, a couple of things stand out as this set came together.

145
00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:40.490
Early discussions had found the need for a separate conference room unnecessary.

146
00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:46.890
Keeping the space more open and less cubicle-like, another contrast from Dunder Mifflin.

147
00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:52.370
Then there were changes in the script that did indeed require for a separate space for

148
00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:54.610
meetings and more private encounters.

149
00:06:55.190 --> 00:07:00.110
Well, there was no such space in this now almost finished and decorated space.

150
00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:01.270
Wow.

151
00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:03.550
They built the whole thing without a conference room.

152
00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:09.430
So all these scenes that happened with Daryl at the interview were kind of like a late-breaking

153
00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:10.270
idea.

154
00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:11.210
That's right.

155
00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:17.130
Matt goes on to say, our wonderful art director, Yvonne, knew every square inch of stage two,

156
00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:21.510
having drafted and laid out numerous configurations of scenery over several seasons.

157
00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:30.730
And she realized that behind one wall of the newly built athlete office was 12 feet of unused space.

158
00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:31.650
Wow.

159
00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:40.770
So if we pushed that wall back and added some sliding glass doors, we could maybe now accommodate

160
00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:41.810
a conference room.

161
00:07:41.930 --> 00:07:47.030
And with the great construction crew and scenic artists we relied on, it could be camera-ready

162
00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:49.070
in under 48 hours.

163
00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:54.750
You know what blows my mind is that most of the scenes at Athlete end up taking place in

164
00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:59.010
this little room that they added in 48 hours.

165
00:07:59.150 --> 00:07:59.290
Yeah.

166
00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:01.290
This extra little 12 feet of space.

167
00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:04.210
Thanks to Yvonne for knowing every square inch of that space.

168
00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:04.530
Yeah.

169
00:08:04.730 --> 00:08:09.650
Matt goes on to say, another addition to the set, which we were mindful of keeping on budget,

170
00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:15.750
was a steel stairway leading to an unseen upper floor of Athlete office space.

171
00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:21.970
Late in the process, my construction coordinator, Tim James, crunched the numbers, and yes, we

172
00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:24.470
could afford to add that element in the background.

173
00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:30.490
Significantly helping increase the sense of scale to the space, and we added some animation

174
00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:32.770
as employees were able to come down the stairs.

175
00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:38.030
It might seem an insignificant element, but we took great pleasure when it was installed,

176
00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:40.690
again, in about less than 48 hours.

177
00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:41.090
Okay.

178
00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:44.410
I completely noticed that staircase.

179
00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:46.270
And I loved it.

180
00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:51.690
And it did feel like, I don't know, everything, it felt bigger.

181
00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:52.670
It felt more important.

182
00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:54.130
It felt more energized.

183
00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:54.690
Yeah.

184
00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:55.710
Wow.

185
00:08:56.110 --> 00:08:57.290
Thank you, Matt Flynn.

186
00:08:57.530 --> 00:08:59.250
We love those behind-the-scenes details.

187
00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:00.550
I loved them.

188
00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:01.390
Thank you, Matt.

189
00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:02.050
And Matt.

190
00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:03.270
Thank you, Matt and Matt.

191
00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:09.410
All right, moving on to fast fact number three, I want to give a big shout-out to Ed

192
00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:14.510
Lauter, who played Mr. Stone of Stone & Son Suit Warehouse.

193
00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:15.550
He was fantastic.

194
00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:18.150
He is a film and television legend.

195
00:09:18.410 --> 00:09:20.930
He is a prolific character actor.

196
00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:23.650
We were so lucky to have him on our show.

197
00:09:23.890 --> 00:09:28.930
He passed away in 2013, and The Office was one of the last things he worked on.

198
00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:34.330
He was also a recurring character on Shameless at the time, and he had filmed a couple of

199
00:09:34.330 --> 00:09:37.290
movies that came out after he had passed away.

200
00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:40.470
He worked as a stand-up comedian before getting into film.

201
00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:46.410
He appeared in more than 200 film and television episodes over his 40-year career.

202
00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:50.090
He was in four films with Charles Bronson.

203
00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:56.090
He was in Death Wish 3, Death Hunt, The White Buffalo, and Break Heart Pass.

204
00:09:56.090 --> 00:10:00.290
He was also in an Alfred Hitchcock film called Family Plot.

205
00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:05.110
And he appeared in three films that were nominated for Best Picture.

206
00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:08.710
He was in Born on the Fourth of July, Seabiscuit, and The ArtiSt.

207
00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:13.890
And I found this interview that he did in 2003, and it had a quote that I was completely

208
00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:14.510
charmed by.

209
00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:16.310
He said, quote,

210
00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:17.470
Someone said to me,

211
00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:19.130
Eddie, you're a turn actor.

212
00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:24.450
That's when a story is going along, and your character shows up, and the story suddenly

213
00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:25.810
takes a major turn.

214
00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:27.030
I love that.

215
00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:28.650
I love that term, turn actor.

216
00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:29.650
Yeah, I do, too.

217
00:10:29.650 --> 00:10:30.250
I've never heard that before.

218
00:10:30.550 --> 00:10:34.990
And you know exactly that moment when you watch a movie when everything shifts.

219
00:10:35.170 --> 00:10:38.910
Like someone comes to town, and there's information dropped, and you're like, uh-oh.

220
00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:39.390
Yeah.

221
00:10:39.710 --> 00:10:41.310
That's a great term.

222
00:10:41.330 --> 00:10:41.870
I love it.

223
00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:46.930
Well, I just wanted to honor him, his body of work, and he was great on our episode.

224
00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:47.390
Yeah.

225
00:10:47.990 --> 00:10:48.370
All right.

226
00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:50.310
Well, that's all I've got for Fast Facts.

227
00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:51.990
Well, why don't we take a break?

228
00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:56.350
Because when we get back, Dwight is going to get a new instant message.

229
00:10:56.550 --> 00:10:57.570
Old tech alert.

230
00:10:57.810 --> 00:11:02.590
It is the oldest instant message I've ever seen.

231
00:11:02.590 --> 00:11:03.050
I know.

232
00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:15.280
We are back.

233
00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:19.640
And like you said, this episode is going to start with some old tech.

234
00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:20.240
Mm-hmm.

235
00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:25.800
Dwight is at his computer, and a bright red message box pops up in the lower right-hand

236
00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:26.220
corner.

237
00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:28.240
Says, new instant message.

238
00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:29.560
It's from David Wallace.

239
00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:35.160
And that stone-and-sun suit warehouse contract with their paper supplier expired.

240
00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:37.500
David Wallace is like, go get it.

241
00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:38.200
Yeah.

242
00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:42.720
Dwight has a talking head where he explains that he and Jim had a tradition whenever they

243
00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:45.740
were pitching to family-owned businesses, they would pretend to be brothers.

244
00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:49.100
And they did this for a law firm.

245
00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:52.380
They did it for a construction company, for a motorcycle store.

246
00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:53.880
I have a costume breakdown.

247
00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:56.020
Oh, yes, because they would dress the part.

248
00:11:56.300 --> 00:11:57.020
They sure did.

249
00:11:57.020 --> 00:12:02.220
For their family-owned law firm, they both wore very nice suits, much nicer than they

250
00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:03.100
normally wear.

251
00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:03.600
Mm-hmm.

252
00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:05.560
And they slicked back their hair.

253
00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:06.160
Yeah.

254
00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:09.920
A little bit of a Gordon Gekko Wall Street moment happening.

